 ## Overview
-Ever Rendering is a real-time volumetric rendering method that uses 3D Gaussian ellipsoids as geometric primitives to represent a consistent 3D radiance field, avoiding the 2D billboard approximation inherent in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Differentiable Ellipsoid Primitive](3490-differentiable-ellipsoid-primitive.md) which may rely on rasterization or different approximations, Ever Rendering replaces rasterization with ray tracing to capture volume density distributions for blending, enabling real-time performance without approximation errors in the transmittance calculation [S1].
+Ever Rendering is a real-time volumetric rendering method that represents a radiance field as a set of constant-density volumetric ellipsoids, avoiding the 2D billboard approximation of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Differentiable Ellipsoid Primitive](3490-differentiable-ellipsoid-primitive.md), it replaces rasterization with ray tracing to capture volume density distributions for blending, enabling exact transmittance calculation without approximation errors [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 ### Inputs and Outputs
 **Inputs:**
-- A set of $N$ 3D Gaussian ellipsoids, each defined by a center position $\mathbf{t}_k$, a rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}_k$, and a scaling vector $\mathbf{s}_k = [s_{kx}, s_{ky}, s_{kz}]^T$.
-- An optimized scalar parameter $\alpha_k \in [0, 1]$ representing the peak opacity along the shortest axis.
-- Color $\mathbf{c}_k$ for each ellipsoid.
-- Camera parameters (position, orientation, intrinsics).
